 "checked_text": "this page answers your question by chaining three rules: slow start grows fast, additive increase grows gently, then timeout triggers multiplicative decrease. i'll circle the three rule headings first.\n\nthe slide gives no concrete numbers, so let's use an example: start cwnd at 1 mss and set the threshold to 8 mss. in slow start, each ack adds 1, so per round the window doubles: 1 to 2 to 4 to 8.\nexample: ssthresh=8\ncwnd 1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 8\n\nat cwnd 8, we have reached the threshold, so slow start stops. follow my arrow down: the next acks use congestion avoidance instead of doubling.\nafter threshold\nno more doubling\n\nnow the rule is gentler: each ack adds 1/n, which totals about +1 mss per rtt. so our example continues 8 to 9 to 10 to 11 to 12 before the timeout hits.\n8 -> 9 -> 10 -> 11 -> 12\n\nat round 8, the timeout occurs while cwnd is 12. this rule halves the threshold to 6, and resets congwin to 1 mss, so slow start begins again.\n12/2=6; cwnd=1\nrestart\n\nwith the new threshold 6, the restarted slow start goes 1 to 2 to 4 to 6, capped at the threshold. so the answer is: cwnd sequence 1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, then timeout resets it to 1.\nnew: 1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 6\ncwnd=1\nslow start\nhit threshold\nadditive increase\ntimeout\ncwnd=1 again\ncwnd=1 -> slow start\nslow start -> hit threshold\nhit threshold -> additive increase\nadditive increase -> timeout\ntimeout -> cwnd=1 again"
